Z||000002||DaRkLoRD||01-27-2001||12:05 AM||||I'm planning to move to Seattle in a few years.. (4-5 years)<P>not for an IT job though.<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000003||Cheever2||01-27-2001||12:12 AM||||I like Houston more all the time, after moving here almost 2 years ago.<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000004||frontal lobe||01-27-2001||12:47 AM||||Well, you left it pretty wide open if you want it a little warmer in winter!<P>I live in suburban Milwaukee and it's really nice.  Probably not like you would imagine from it's reputation.  Lake Michigan, lots of small lakes nearby, several universities to attract young people, professional basketball and (sort of)baseball.  Bad taxes in the state, though.  Bummer.  LOTS of nice restaurants.  Plenty of IT work.<P>When I was sick of winter about 5 years ago and toyed with moving, I picked up a "Places Rated Almanac".  All kinds of useful information about different places in our great country.<P>A little flag waving here, but despite many faults, this is a GREAT country to live in.  <P>If you ever want to check Milwaukee out, I'll put you up for a couple nights if you want (no smoking, though).||||reg||1
Z||000005||stimpy||01-27-2001||12:54 AM||||Well, it sounds an awful lot like you are describing the SF Bay Area in beautiful California. My suggestion would be check out my former home of Tracy CA. Somewhat affordable housing (relative to Silicon Valley prices), temperate climate (ok, hot in summer, foggy in winter, but no snow!) and within a couple hours drive from anything from surfing Santa Cruz to skiing Tahoe. Sacramento, San Jose, San Francisco, Berkely and Oakland all within 1 hr drive.<BR>Jobs? The east bay is home to several large high tech employers, including PeopleSoft in Pleasanton (20 min commute) which has an extremely laid-back employee oriented atmosphere. (Jeans ok everyday, bring your dog to work!) <BR> <BR>Be prepared for some sticker shock if you want to move to Northern Cali, though. In Tracy, an average 2bd 1 ba apartment goes for $650-$750.  In San Jose, you can double that.||||reg||1
Z||000006||onfire||01-27-2001||04:38 AM||||I would say Seattle with all the tech jobs. Lots to do, and cool scenery. But i may be biased. I love it here It only snowed for about 20 min. this year. But if you go for an hour drive you can be knee deep in the snow. The only things that suck are traffic, and the people that still don't know how to drive in the rain.||||reg||1
Z||000007||onfire||01-27-2001||04:41 AM||||Oh yeah they are having a BIG tech job fair in the Convention Center this weekend (I think) if you can make it. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||onfire||01-27-2001||04:48 AM||||Need a job check this out. <A HREF="http://www.nwhtjobs.com/seattlecompanies.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.nwhtjobs.com/seattlecompanies.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Raydar||01-27-2001||12:36 PM||||Ever think about Atlanta or surrounds?<BR>It's gonna be in the 50's today. <BR>Snow is fairly uncommon. Gets hot in the summer. Mid-upper 90's. Kinda humid. Beach is 4-6 hours away. About 8 hours to Daytona. Mountains are 2-3 hours away. Fiero Factory is 4 hours away   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>IT jobs out the wazoo, as it's a major communications hub. (Go to monster.com and do a job search for Atlanta)<BR>Great baseball team. (Braves)<BR>NO football team. (Falcons)<BR>Stuff to do? Plenty. Major party scene in the North burbs and Buckhead. Downtown is a ghost town at night, except for the GA Dome and Phillips Arena. <BR>Housing in the suburbs (30-40 miles from town) can be had for 100-150K. That's for a 3 br., 2 bath house on 1/2 acre, with a drive-under garage. In town condos start at 180 or so. Most have secure parking.<BR>Talk to Travis, too. He lives Northwest of the city. I live West.  <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 01-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||bHooper||01-27-2001||01:44 PM||||well, let me step in there for North Carolina!<P>We have everything that you could probably ask for... ocean, mountains, water skiing, snow skiing, ice/roller hockey, football, basketball, golf (some of the best golf courses in the wrold), fishing of all kinds, and some great looking women!<P>As for jobs, there are a lot of tech jobs around the state.  Near Raleigh is a place called Research Triangle Park, it's loaded with tech jobs.<P>Hope this helps some!<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000011||FieroLisa||01-27-2001||02:51 PM||||Yes, North Carolina is very nice- but if I could live anywhere it would be Arizona, or maybe even Colorado, but that gets cold in the winter- but its a gorgeous snowy cold! I just want a big farm for my horses out west somewhere.<P>------------------<BR>Lisa<BR>86 gold 4 speed GT<BR>86 gold 5 speed SC (Sold!)<BR>Columbus, OH/Greensboro NC<BR>AOL IM: FieroLisa<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>FieroLisa's Website</A><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/pffsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||GT Bastard||01-27-2001||08:53 PM||||I'm pretty psyched about moving... Maybe I'll end up staying right here for now, but I just want something to happen and it's not happening on it's own.  I actually saw a special on Atlanta on some channel a few days ago and it looked really cool.  If I could land a job, i'd probably go for it...  Thanks for all the input guys.  Frontal Lobe, thanks for the offer!  ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Cliff Pennock||01-27-2001||09:23 PM||||I liked San Antonio a lot. The riverwalk is beautiful at night and if you like warm weather, then this is the place for you.<P>Oh - and the best fajitas of the country!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||RiceCooker||01-27-2001||09:26 PM||||Move up north, Eh!<P>||||reg||6
Z||000015||LarryB||01-27-2001||09:37 PM||||If you're looking for an IT job there are 4 areas you want to take a good hard look at, in no particular order:<P>metro Boston<BR>Research Triangle (Raleigh/Durham NC area)<BR>Silicon Valley<BR>metro Denver<P>All of them have warmer and less snowy winters than South Dakota, all of them have decent recreation within a couple of hours drive (especially Boston and Research Triangle IMO). Housing costs are probably the most in Silicon Valley, the least in NC, with Boston and Denver now about the same. (housing costs near Denver has skyrocketed the last few years)<P>Houston and Atlanta are too hot in the summer for my taste. San Jose isn't much better. (I grew up in Maine so I'm biased on that front.)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000016||sqoach||01-28-2001||12:47 AM||||ooooooooooooklahoma where the wind comes sweeping down the plains.....blah blah blah blah.<P>definately warmer than where you are. it's pretty cold right now though. we're getting the nasty winter weather.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||PatrickB||01-28-2001||02:14 AM||||Long Island is a really good place for IT jobs.  A lot of the companies around here and not enough IT people.  Headquarters to alot of places: symbol, periphonics/nortel networks, Computer Associates, Bay networks.  You can tell how desperate a lot of companies are by the kind of recruiting they do.  You can also be close to NYC<P>Weather is okay.  Not too cold in winter and not too hot in summer since you are living on an island surrounded by water.<P>Taxes suck though depending on where you live...||||reg||1
Z||000018||CityChick||01-28-2001||03:02 AM||||Las Vegas is a really nice place!  It gets a little too warm in a summer, but it's a good place to be from August to March!||||reg||11
Z||000019||My7Fieros||01-28-2001||08:33 PM||||Not here, thats for sure. Memphis is a sespool of filth and crime. You have no idea how well you've got it in SD..believe me, you may think Im crazy (Ive been there several times), but spend a month in "Memfrica", escpecially during the summer, and you'll be BEGGING to go back to SD!<BR>Now, on a lighter note, I hear San Diego is a nice place, although I think Ive had it up to my eyeballs with hearing about California (no offense to the Cali members here)..<BR>Most places in New England are nice..Id like to move to Vermont or somewhere like that..low crime, beautiful seasons (escpecially fall).....ahhhhhhh  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
