Z||000050||stsmithgt||02-07-2001||04:57 PM||||Well,,,,,There's a Silicone Valley developing north of Sacramento near Auburn CA.  That's where I think I'm going to end up.  I'd rather visit Monterey CA. than work here.  You get all four seasons.  It's nice to have change in the weather.  The ocean is an hour and a half away.  The water skiing is minutes away.  The snow skiing is a couple hours away along with gambling.  I don't mind the humidity in Arkansas when I visit my Dad, but living there? Nah.  Only dust devils around here.  All the trailer parks are pretty safe from tornadoes. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tornado_e0.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> With a little geological stratagy, you can avoid flooding or earthquake damage easy enough.  When a gate broke on the Folsom Lake Dam, alls that did is make our rafting down the river go faster.(and drown a few folks that wear boots while in a rushing river.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> Darwinizm at it's best.) Oh well, that's enough.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/penny_spin_md_clr.gif">     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/penny_spin_md_clr.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by stsmithgt (edited 02-08-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000051||MrPBody||02-07-2001||09:01 PM||||20 years ago, I would have said that Atlanta is a <I>great</I> place to live!  It's still a good place, but now there are too damned many cars and people driving them.  And general cost of living has gone way up.<P>On the other hand, the ocean is 4 or 5 hours away, mountains are 2 hours north, and today's high temperature was in the 60's.<P>And restaurants?  God, the restaurants!  A great place to get fat.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000052||Robert||02-07-2001||11:18 PM||||Being one of 4 people that are natives of San Francisco, I vote for the anywhere near the San Francisco Bay Area.  Sure my two-bedroom house cost $375,000, and a gallon of gas is near $2.00, and the lights go out once a week; but other than that it’s great to live here. I can drive to the snow at Tahoe in less than 3 hours, (80mph+), or I can hit the beach in 30 min.  I’m only 20 min from the Fiero Shop.  It ‘s been 60 degrees out lately, and my wife and I are playing in a tennis tournament this weekend. <BR>What else could you ask for?<BR>A job maybe…….?<BR>We just ran an ad for a graphic designer at my printing shop and got over 30 responses.  It’s tough out there with all the .com’s going under.<BR><P>------------------<BR>          <IMG SRC="http://canterburytrails.com/photo/T2.GIF"> <P> “If you don’t look for that parking space near the entrance, you’ll never get it”||||reg||10
Z||000053||Philphine||02-08-2001||10:40 AM||||louisville ends up in a lot of top 10-20 best city polls. i think in one it was about 3. but, living here i, guess that just means it's really average, or what people take for average. haven't done alot of traveling so i can't make real comparisions. ||||reg||1
Z||000054||danny boy||02-09-2001||06:02 PM||||Stay the HELL away from West by god Virginia... I used to drive a truck so Ive seen a lot of the country. my personal favorite place is either Elizabethtown,KY or Evansville,IN. both are pretty nice. I couldnt stand any state that I-95 ran through or anywhere in New England. Come to think of it, Anything east of interstate 81 is not for me. If our whole eastern seaboard fell into the ocean,I wouldnt feel bad a bit.<BR>I like it here but there is just no jobs.||||reg||1
Z||000055||2birds||02-11-2001||11:57 PM||||I grew up in Eastern Iowa.  I'd go back there in a heartbeat, even with the cold winters.  Good schools, nice people, seasons (five in Cedar Rapids  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">), still cheap to live there.  I left in 1985 because the economy sucked, but it's a lot better now.<P>In my 15 years in the Navy, I've been in<BR>- North Chicago (didn't get out much)<BR>- Orlando (lots to do, nice weather, getting expensive<BR>- Albany, NY area (left before the winter, nice rural area, but pricey)<BR>- Norfolk, VA (always something to do there, but crime and traffic were pretty bad)<BR>- Rochester, MN (just like Iowa, only a bit colder  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> )<BR>- Silverdale, WA (across from Seattle, expensive and wet, too much flannel for me)<BR>- Groton, CT (cold winter, cold people, and EXPENSIVE)<BR>- The SE coast of Georgia, where I live now, is cheap, but so are wages, generally.  Small town attitudes, but generally very nice people.  Jacksonville, FL is only a half hour away, so I have access to anything I need.   ||||reg||1
Z||000056||Fformula88||02-14-2001||12:01 AM||||HEY!  I am not the only person dying to get away from this cold and snowy hell I live in!  Today's USA Today had an article in it proclaiming Buffalo as the city with the most heart in the country!  Ok, I can agree with that.  When the weather is bad, or you are in need of help people are always willing to pitch in.  While stalled out on a thruway ramp, I had 5 people stop in ten minutes to offer the use of their cell phones to call for help.  But trust me, the 150+ inches of snow per winter gets tiring VERY quickly.  I have traveled all over the country and have decided the best place seems to live seems to be Pheonix, despite the hot summer temps.  Sunshine is everywhere, and sunshine has a way of making you happier.  I hate Buffalo's grayness in the winter.  I am hoping to move to Pheonix and have started my job search there.  80A would be an awesome road to drive a Fiero on north of Pheonix!  Lots of twisties!  hehe.  I also really like the Washington DC area, but its way too expensive there.  Chicago is fun, but I find it a little too crowded.  I find Florida too touristy.  Hawaii was the best place I have ever been, but I need to be able to drive my Fiero all over the country!  So living on an island wouldn't be good for the long term.  New England is very pretty, but also kinda pricey.  Especially Boston.||||reg||1
